  "query_preview": "..\n\nBenign weather expected through the end of the week characterized by \ndry conditions and above normal temperatures. Northwest periphery of \nhigh pressure centered over the Appalachia region will support stable \nconditions under clear skies for the remainder of the day. \nTemperatures which have tapped the low 60s in many spots will follow \nthe typical diurnal swing into the low 40s overnight. As high \npressure begins to wash out into the western Atlantic tomorrow, low \npressure will proceed across the northern Plains into Ontario. This \nwill strengthen the pressure gradient across the Great Lakes through \nthe day elevating gradient flow with stronger winds building in \naloft. This will bring some breezy conditions through tomorrow with \ngusts ranging between 20 to 30 mph. The stronger gusts towards 30 \nmph will be more probable across the northern half of the cwa given \nslightly better mixing depths tapping into these stronger winds \naloft relative to southern counterparts (1.5 kft vs 2.5kft). As low \npressure departs into Quebec, high pressure will fill back in on \nFriday which will maintain dry weather. There will be a low chance \nfor patchy fog or haze development Friday morning closer to sunrise \nbut light winds aoa 5 kt does not bring high confidence for coverage \nbeyond patchy wording. A very similar pattern relative to today will \nthen again like play out with high pressure washing out across the \ncontinental northeast as low pressure traverses the southern \nCanadian provinces. This will strengthen the pressure gradient \nacross the Great Lakes which will likely bring in the warmest \ntemperatures of the week with the boost in waa noted by 850mb \ntemperatures peaking towards 11C. Temperatures each day this week \nwill likely peak into the low 60s with Saturday potentially making a \npush into the mid 60s. \n\nA large pattern shift to then commence on Sunday as a couple of \nwaves, one wave/trough across the Rockies and a second wave pushing \nin across the northern Plains, aids in pushing a cold front across \nthe Great Lakes. Gulf moisture just ahead of the frontal boundary \nwill stream in over SE MI bringing increasing chances for rain \nshowers through the day with forcing along the front carrying rain \nchances into the evening hours. Cold air advection in the wake of \nthe front will bring temperatures back to more normal values \ncharacterized by highs in the upper 30s to low 40s and lows in the \n20s.",
